    "summary": "First live Ripple & Brokedown Palace; strong acoustic/electric flow",
    "blurb": "The set opens with the debut of Ripple and Brokedown Palace, then rolls into a tight electric half featuring Truckin' and an inspired Cryptical Envelopment > The Other One suite. Jerry’s guitar swells and classic vocal work stand out while the rhythm section stays rock‑steady. Several audience tapes and an excellent matrix capture preserve this historic performance.",
    "review": "On August 18, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead delivered a landmark show that introduced three future staples – “Ripple,” “Brokedown Palace” and the first live version of “Truckin’.” The acoustic opener is a treat, with Jerry’s guitar tone described as “amazing” and his volume‑knob swells on “Attics Of My Life” adding extra color. The electric set maintains momentum, highlighted by a fiery Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One sequence and a soulful rendition of “Operator.”  While the original lo‑fi audience tape is only fair, an excellent multi‑channel matrix capture (gd1970-08-18.141071.aud.pitch-adjusted.flac1644) provides direct acoustic clarity and a vivid 7‑channel mix for the electric portion, making it the best listening source. Overall the band is locked in: Phil lays down a steady bass foundation, Bill keeps solid time with occasional flourishes from Mickey, and Pigpen’s piano (or possible guest Howard Wales/Ned Lagin) adds texture on several numbers.",
